{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And the land shall yield her fruit.</b> The meaning is: the fruit designated within her power to produce — for even though the fruit of the land appears, one cannot assess it, for this is the power of the land and no more; since you find (Ketubot 112b) that the Sage rebuked the land and it restrained its power and did not yield its fruits afterward in the same order as before, and one who sees its fruits after his rebuke — not before — would not consider [them extraordinary], for this is its power." "<b>And you shall eat to satiety.</b> Perhaps, since He said ״וְנָתְנָה הָאָרֶץ פִּרְיָהּ״ — which is bounded at the outer limit in the richness of the fruits and the taste of the fruits — for this reason a person cannot eat from them to the point of satiety, and this is something felt according to nature. Therefore HaShem promised them that the fruits would be such that they would eat from them to satiety." "<b>And you shall dwell securely, etc.</b> And even though He already said in the preceding verse ״וִישַׁבְתֶּם עַל הָאָרֶץ לָבֶטַח״, He returned to say so, to promise that the abundance of praise of the fruits would not bring a robber upon them. And therefore He was precise in saying in the first verse ״וִישַׁבְתֶּם עַל הָאָרֶץ״ — to say that this assurance concerns the desirability of the land, and the second assurance concerns the desirability of its fruits; and in this they are guaranteed both the land and its fruits."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:13.990364738Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}